WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.526
(dramatic music)

00:00:01.526 --> 00:00:05.099
- Searching for a ship
or airplane in the ocean

00:00:05.099 --> 00:00:08.960
is technically more challenging
than going into space.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.456
You're essentially dealing with
one atmosphere of pressure.

00:00:12.456 --> 00:00:16.568
When we go to 6,000 meters,
we're dealing with pressures

00:00:16.568 --> 00:00:18.318
that would crush you.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:28.728
I'm Steve Saint Amour, the
co-founder of Eclipse Group

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.276
and a subsea explorer.

00:00:30.276 --> 00:00:34.377
We specialize in ultra deep
water search and recovery.

00:00:34.377 --> 00:00:38.317
When we go out on an operation,
we find crashed ships

00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:40.476
or airplanes in the ocean.

00:00:40.476 --> 00:00:44.013
Then upon finding them,
we recover the object.

00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:47.180
Over the course of my 32 year career,

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.974
I participated in 100 plus high profile

00:00:50.974 --> 00:00:55.092
search and recovery
operations, like Air France 447

00:00:55.092 --> 00:00:57.935
and a number of military aircraft.

00:00:57.935 --> 00:01:00.416
The reason these investigations occur is

00:01:00.416 --> 00:01:03.909
to prevent the future loss of life.

00:01:03.909 --> 00:01:06.714
Hello Horizon, Steve Saint Amour speaking.

00:01:06.714 --> 00:01:09.923
The first step is
receiving the phone call.

00:01:09.923 --> 00:01:12.899
When we get the call,
we go out with the ship,

00:01:12.899 --> 00:01:16.370
we deploy the side scan
sonar to search the area

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.857
where we think the object is.

00:01:18.857 --> 00:01:22.271
Upon finding it, we would deploy
the remote operated vehicle

00:01:22.271 --> 00:01:25.335
which would go down
and recover the object,

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:29.366
and all the information that's
collected by the vehicle

00:01:29.366 --> 00:01:32.048
you're getting the results of
that information in real-time.

00:01:32.048 --> 00:01:36.114
And after everything that
we have to go through

00:01:36.114 --> 00:01:40.991
to assemble the equipment,
go out, perform a search,

00:01:40.991 --> 00:01:43.345
when it finally breaks
the surface of the water,

00:01:43.345 --> 00:01:45.417
it's a phenomenal feeling.

00:01:45.417 --> 00:01:48.107
It's even better when we get
it up to the deck of the ship.

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:52.274
The proof is there that
you've done your job right.

00:01:54.966 --> 00:01:56.553
As a kid, you want to make a difference.

00:01:56.553 --> 00:01:59.235
You want to find and, you know, discover new things

00:01:59.235 --> 00:02:01.472
and tell people about them,

00:02:01.472 --> 00:02:04.639
and that really was, you know, the driving force.

00:02:05.707 --> 00:02:06.966
I dreamt of spending the rest of my life

00:02:06.966 --> 00:02:09.482
exploring the oceans.

00:02:09.482 --> 00:02:12.141
Now I'm living my dream and every day is

00:02:12.141 --> 00:02:15.141
a unique challenge and an adventure.

